 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "All of this lives in `src/tessel/views.py`, except the route registration.\n\n**Turning the class into a view function**\n- `View.to_view_func(name, *class_args, **class_kwargs)` (`views.py:86`) is a classmethod that returns a plain function `view(**kwargs)`.\n- On each call, `view` creates an instance of the class and runs `active_app.ensure_sync(self.run_view)(**kwargs)`.\n- If `init_every_request` is false, one shared instance is created up front instead.\n- It applies any `decorators`.\n- It attaches `view_class`, `methods` and `provide_automatic_options` to the function, so registration can read them.\n\n**Routing HTTP methods to handlers**\n- `VerbView(View)` (`views.py:138`) is the class that does this.\n- `VerbView.__init_subclass__` (`views.py:165`) scans for attributes named in `http_method_funcs`, such as `get` and `post`. It sets `cls.methods = {\"GET\", \"POST\"}`, merged with any base-class methods.\n- `VerbView.run_view` (`views.py:182`) does `getattr(self, request.method.lower())` and calls it with the URL kwargs. A HEAD request with no `head` method falls back to `get`.\n\n**Registration**\n- `app.attach_route_rule(path, view_func=Cls.to_view_func(\"name\"))`, implemented at `src/tessel/sansio/app.py:611`, reads `view_func.methods` and `view_func.provide_automatic_options`.\n- It builds the URL rule with those methods, and adds OPTIONS automatically when the rule doesn't already allow it.",
